 "relevance_note": "One patient in this WES cohort of suspected mitochondrial disease carries an ALDH5A1 variant: Table 2 row '17/M ... ALDH5A1 | c.515G>A, p.(Arg172His) homozygous | Succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase deficiency'.",
 "method_note": "Read by hand; no script transcribed a table (a script only assembled the JSON). Only patient 17 of Table 2 is in scope; the other 65 cohort members (other genes or undiagnosed) get no rows; cohort size 66. Label: the 'Patient no./sex' column holds bare indexing numbers with sex ('17/M') and the text never names patient 17, so 'Table 2 #17' (the paper calls other patients 'patient 10', 'patient 11' in text). Table 2 columns: Patient no./sex, Family history, Age of onset/age of last follow-up, Initial presentation, Muscular, CNS, Heart, Vision, Hearing, Neuro-imaging, MDC score (pre-biopsy), Gene, Variant, Disease association. The table legend does not define '+' or '−'; '+' in CNS and Neuro-imaging recorded as observed (organ-system involvement / abnormal neuroimaging, consistent with the text 'of these patients, 50 had abnormal neuroimaging features'), and the '−' cells (Family history, Muscular, Heart, Vision, Hearing) are treated as not reported because the symbol is undefined. No checklist item is specific enough to be set from 'CNS +' or 'Neuro-imaging +'. Vital status left 'not reported': the row lacks the '^a' age-at-death marker, so 9 yo is presumably age at last follow-up, but survival is not stated. Ethnicity 'Chinese' and consanguinity 'no' come from the cohort-wide statement that all recruited patients were from non-consanguineous Chinese families. Pathogenicity 'disease-causing' comes from the Table 2 title, which applies to every variant in the table. Variant type not stated (not derived from HGVS). MDC score 8, definite; initial presentation non-syndromic (not recorded as features). cites_prior_report is true only for COQ4 patients 1 to 4 (ref [16]), who are out of scope. Cohort-level feature counts (e.g. GDD 33/66) assign nothing to patient 17. No contradictions found.",
